                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "6183aaa7-f171-4fa9-a687-bf3950394f1d",
                        "source_entry_id": "731f7379-5dab-333a-97cd-7cd1e8e43ca6",
                        "source_sense_id": "34788632-4adf-3e85-8077-aa94aed75d06",
                        "target_lang_word": "octadecahedron",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "octadecahedron",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "5bb81caa-3612-3b27-938a-ce701aeb4bf4",
                                        "entry_id": "74e4a86f-93de-37f8-86f8-4606dbed574d",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A polyhedron with eighteen faces."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "08be5cf0-a507-4047-9ec8-dac72db60f77",
                                                "sense_id": "5bb81caa-3612-3b27-938a-ce701aeb4bf4",
                                                "text": "The cell structure was considered to consist of spherical holes but for the purposes of calculation, the cells were assumed to be octadecahedra. ln this model, a compressive force along the principal axis of the octadecahedron and parallel to."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "8abbb949-6236-4a6c-8c37-0254485bf13f",
                                                "sense_id": "5bb81caa-3612-3b27-938a-ce701aeb4bf4",
                                                "text": "In fact the structure of 2 can be related to the octadecahedron and the pentacapped trigonal prism in an interesting fashion"
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "8f352726-33e0-49d0-aac3-845c0deab667",
                        "source_entry_id": "731f7379-5dab-333a-97cd-7cd1e8e43ca6",
                        "source_sense_id": "34788632-4adf-3e85-8077-aa94aed75d06",
                        "target_lang_word": "octakaidecahedron",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "octakaidecahedron",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "c0618b38-cdcc-3c3e-a116-d12c256a3942",
                                        "entry_id": "e7e2350d-0f42-351f-8c0b-9927739fdbf6",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Synonym of octadecahedron."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "f882abc5-dd6a-4b97-8d5a-45bee290709f",
                                                "sense_id": "c0618b38-cdcc-3c3e-a116-d12c256a3942",
                                                "text": "The compound is a clathrate hydrate, and the most notable feature of the water framework is a novel polyhedral cage, the 18-hedron (octakaidecahedron),[…]"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "6caf24c1-bb90-4d31-b7ac-817e587cdc7a",
                                                "sense_id": "c0618b38-cdcc-3c3e-a116-d12c256a3942",
                                                "text": "The most interesting feature here is the appearance of a new type of polyhedral cage, the 18-hedron or octakaidecahedron (see Fig. 115)."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "8becd6e2-000b-4cc6-a091-869e17ebcc41",
                                                "sense_id": "c0618b38-cdcc-3c3e-a116-d12c256a3942",
                                                "text": "The d-functions adapted to spherical symmetry divide the sphere in six quadrangles (around the three Cartesian axes) reserved for the e_g sub-shell and twelve hexagons for the t_(2g) sub-shell very similar to a cubic octakaidecahedron projected out to the sphere."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "7fd4115c-6291-496c-ae7d-a23a2d7d56f6",
                                                "sense_id": "c0618b38-cdcc-3c3e-a116-d12c256a3942",
                                                "text": "This is a packing of cubes and octakaidecahedra in which there are two types of grain comer geometry."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "453df94b-39dd-423d-97da-2ba05607e4ae",
                                                "sense_id": "c0618b38-cdcc-3c3e-a116-d12c256a3942",
                                                "text": "The rhombic faces of one-third of the rhombicdodecahedra move outwards, resulting in octakaidecahedra (8), which are equivalent to, but larger than, those of figure 2.6(b).[…]If the square faces of the octakaidecahedra continue to grow, thereby shrinking the rhombohexagonal dodecahedra, a simple cubic unimodular structure survives as shown in figure 2.6(e)."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "85b5ad1d-8235-4b14-b6d2-734bc9e84f70",
                                                "sense_id": "c0618b38-cdcc-3c3e-a116-d12c256a3942",
                                                "text": "It is an octakaidecahedron consisting of a pair of (004) square faces[…]"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "89f496b3-ecef-4f60-aff4-c96a1efb18a6",
                                                "sense_id": "c0618b38-cdcc-3c3e-a116-d12c256a3942",
                                                "text": "[…]so that the two can be regarded as single face, with the periphery more accurately described as a polyhedron with 12 vertices and 18 faces, an octakaidecahedron[…]"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "7152c323-7cf6-4b5e-b812-338c9606f11a",
                                                "sense_id": "c0618b38-cdcc-3c3e-a116-d12c256a3942",
                                                "text": "In the present case, it is an octakaidecahedron with 12 vertices and 18 faces.[…]formed by the oxygen atoms of the peripheral octakaidecahedron,[…]"
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
